  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 1124 AM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nBetter rain chances move in on Saturday into Saturday night as\nstronger shortwave energy moves in, along with greater moisture.\nThere is a 50-70% chance for showers and storms, but coverage\nlooks to be scattered and all day washouts are not expected.\nWhile some areas (particularly the far NW CWA) may have some\nareas that stay dry or see very little rainfall, other areas\nhave seen a slight uptick in forecast precip amounts. Still,\namounts will be light at 0.05-0.25 inches. A few thunderstorms\nwill also be possible. Look for highs in the low to mid 70s\nSaturday.\n\nMuch of Sunday will be dry with highs in the 70s.\n\nStill watching the potential for a strong system to move through\nthe region early to middle of next week. Model spread is still \nsignificant with this system, with difference in all aspects of \nthe system. Ensembles show surface low track locations ranging \nfrom northern Texas to the far northern CONUS. Ensemble clusters\nare showing little preference for one scenario over another, \nwith differences in timing, strength, and track, made worse by \nmultiple pieces of energy that may or may not phase and impact \nthe system further. Outcomes generally range from a strong \nclosed low moving into the area (worst case: Widespread rain, \nthunderstorms, maybe a severe and flooding risk) to a weaker, \npositively tiled upper wave (best case: Scattered showers and \nstorms). Current NBM chances for showers and thunderstorms peak \nat 70-80%. Timing of greatest chances is currently Monday night \ninto Tuesday, but that may change. Confidence is too low to \ndiscuss specifics, but those with interest should continue to \nmonitor.",
